{
 "about": "Shared test cases for shifts (P0 A1 to A3, A6, A13). `overlay` defines how effective cards are computed from base cards and shift entries; the page code (P5a) is tested against it, and Python only checks that every patch in it passes validate_patch (it never applies one). `patch_validation` is checked by Python against validate_patch.",
 "overlay_rules": [
  "A case gives base cards, shift entries, a map from each beat id to its chapter number, and the chapter the state is wanted for.",
  "The wanted state is the one going INTO that chapter: an entry for chapter N is applied only when the wanted chapter is greater than N.",
  "Entries are applied in chapter order, then in the order they are listed (recorded). An entry applies to the card of its kind: `characters` to the base character whose id is its target, `world` and `entanglements` to that card.",
  "A scalar field in a patch replaces the value. A list field holds operations, applied in order: `add` appends {id, ...values} at the end; `replace` merges its values into the item with that id, keeping the item's other keys and its place; `remove` deletes the item with that id.",
  "A `replace` or `remove` of an id that is not in the list (never added, or already removed) changes nothing, and neither does it stop the rest of the patch. An `add` of an id that is already there is not defined here and is not tested.",
  "Base cards are abbreviated: they hold only the fields a case uses (every list item keeps its id). The expected cards are in the same abbreviated form, for every card the case gives as base, with keys in the same order. A card with no shifts is returned as it was."
 ],
 "overlay": [
